{"v":3,"iid":"i6","stillW":328,"style":"[hidden] { display: none !important; } .pz { max-width: 62rem; margin-inline: auto; } .pz > *, #stepbox > * { max-width: 30rem; margin-inline: auto; } .pz > #stepbox, #stepbox > .live { max-width: 62rem; } .pz > * + *, #stepbox > * + * { margin-top: 14px; } .pz .k-stepper .k-label, .pz .k-stepper-nav { display: none; } .pz .count { margin-top: 10px; } .task { font-size: 1.2rem; font-weight: 700; line-height: 1.45; } .task.show { font-size: 1.06rem; font-weight: 400; line-height: 1.6; } .task.show.ask { font-weight: 700; } .task > * + * { margin-top: 10px; } .task .card { padding: 10px 12px; border-radius: 10px; background: var(--k-panel); border: 1px solid var(--k-line); line-height: 1.5; } .task .card b, .task .warn-note b { font-weight: 700; } .task .warn-note { line-height: 1.5; } .sub { font-size: .95rem; color: var(--k-muted); line-height: 1.5; } .why { line-height: 1.6; } .live { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 19rem), 1fr)); gap: 14px 28px; align-items: start; } .lcol > * + *, .rcol > * + * { margin-top: 12px; } #outs { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 8px; } #outs .k-readout { padding: 8px 8px 10px 10px; } #outs .k-readout-value { font-size: 1.1rem; } #outs .k-readout.hot .k-readout-value { color: var(--k-warn); } .work { padding: 10px 12px; border-radius: 10px; background: var(--k-panel); border: 1px solid var(--k-line); } .work .wh { margin: 0; font-weight: 700; } .work .wl { margin: 6px 0 0; line-height: 1.5; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; } .fig { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; } .fig .lb { font: 500 13px var(--k-font); fill: var(--k-ink); } .fig .hd { font: 700 13px var(--k-font); fill: var(--k-ink); } .fig .mu { font: 500 13px var(--k-font); fill: var(--k-muted); } .fig .wt { font: 500 13px var(--k-font); fill: var(--k-warn); } .fig .w { fill: none; stroke: var(--k-ink); stroke-width: 1.6; stroke-linecap: round; stroke-linejoin: round; } .fig .plt { stroke: var(--k-ink); stroke-width: 2; } .fig .plt2 { stroke: var(--k-ink); stroke-width: 5; } .fig .coilF { fill: none; stroke: var(--k-ink); stroke-width: 4.5; stroke-linecap: round; } .fig .ia { fill: var(--k-accent2); } .fig .arm { fill: var(--k-sunk); stroke: var(--k-strong); stroke-width: 1.5; } .fig .slot { fill: var(--k-muted); } .fig .brush { fill: var(--k-strong); } .fig .gear { fill: var(--k-panel); stroke: var(--k-strong); stroke-width: 1.4; stroke-linejoin: round; } .fig .hub { fill: var(--k-strong); } .fig .drum { fill: var(--k-panel); stroke: var(--k-ink); stroke-width: 1.6; } .fig .rope { stroke: var(--k-ink); stroke-width: 2; } .fig .turn { fill: none; stroke: var(--k-accent2); stroke-width: 2.5; stroke-linecap: round; } .fig .emf { stroke: var(--k-accent); stroke-width: 2.5; stroke-linecap: round; } .fig .emfh { fill: var(--k-accent); } .fig .barO { fill: none; stroke: var(--k-strong); stroke-width: 1.2; } .fig .barF { fill: var(--k-accent2); } .fig .barE { fill: var(--k-accent); } .fig .ax { fill: none; stroke: var(--k-strong); stroke-width: 1.2; } .fig .grid { stroke: var(--k-line); stroke-width: 1; } .fig .band { fill: var(--k-warn); fill-opacity: .12; } .fig .dl { stroke: var(--k-warn); stroke-width: 1.5; stroke-dasharray: 5 4; } .fig .sp { fill: none; stroke: var(--k-accent2); stroke-width: 3; stroke-linejoin: round; stroke-linecap: round; } .fig .tq { fill: none; stroke: var(--k-accent); stroke-width: 3; stroke-linejoin: round; stroke-linecap: round; } .fig .gd { stroke: var(--k-muted); stroke-width: 1.2; stroke-dasharray: 3 4; } .fig .dotS { fill: var(--k-accent2); stroke: var(--k-panel); stroke-width: 2; } .fig .dotS.hot { fill: var(--k-warn); } .fig .dotT { fill: var(--k-accent); stroke: var(--k-panel); stroke-width: 1.5; } .fig .ldr { stroke: var(--k-muted); stroke-width: 1.2; } .fig .body { fill: var(--k-panel); stroke: var(--k-strong); stroke-width: 1.5; } .fig .jn { fill: var(--k-ink); } .fig .hdl { fill: var(--k-strong); } .fig .lock { fill: var(--k-warn); } .fig .shk { fill: none; stroke: var(--k-strong); stroke-width: 3; } .fig .tag { fill: var(--k-panel); stroke: var(--k-warn); stroke-width: 2; } .fig .term { fill: var(--k-bg); stroke: var(--k-ink); stroke-width: 1.6; } .picks { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px; } .picks .k-btn { width: 100%; min-height: 56px; height: auto; font-size: 1.02rem; line-height: 1.35; white-space: normal; text-align: left; padding: 10px 14px; } .picks .k-btn.is-right { border-color: var(--k-good); color: var(--k-good); box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--k-good); } .picks .k-btn.is-wrong { border-color: var(--k-warn); color: var(--k-warn); } .row { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 10px; } .row .k-btn { flex: 1 1 9rem; min-height: 48px; } .fb { padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 12px; background: var(--k-panel); border: 1.5px solid var(--k-line); line-height: 1.5; } .fb.ok { box-shadow: inset 4px 0 0 var(--k-good); } .fb.bad { box-shadow: inset 4px 0 0 var(--k-warn); } .fb > * + * { margin-top: 6px; } .fb .v { font-weight: 700; font-size: 1.05rem; } .fb.ok .v { color: var(--k-good); } .fb .ans { font-weight: 700; } #stepbox.enter { animation: screen-in .35s ease-out both; } .fb { animation: fade-in .25s ease-out both; } @keyframes fade-in { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } } @keyframes screen-in { from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(4px); } to { opacity: 1; transform: none; } } html.k-testing .fb, html.k-testing #stepbox.enter { animation: none; } @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { #stepbox.enter, .fb { animation: none; } }","pages":[{"kind":"summary","title":"The DC series motor","for":"Loads that need big starting torque and stay coupled. One current feeds field and armature. So torque climbs steeply with current. Take the load off and it runs away.","rule":"One current through field and armature.\nBack-emf: E = V − I R. R is the armature plus the series field. Zero at standstill.\nBelow saturation: torque goes with I².\nIgnoring resistance: speed goes with 1 ÷ I.\nPast saturation: torque goes with I.\nNo starter: I = V ÷ R. 260 V ÷ 0.4 Ω = 650 A, about twelve times full load. The field's resistance makes the real figure a little less.\n\nVery high starting torque. Poor speed regulation.\nA starting resistance limits the starting current, typically to 125 to 200% of full load. Some aim for two to three times. Use the maker's setting.\nReverse it by swapping the field or the armature leads, not both.","traps":[{"text":"Never run it unloaded or drive through a belt. Couple it directly or through gears.","cite":"[^14] [^7] [^16] EDN; IDC"},{"text":"Lose the load and it runs away. It must have overspeed protection.","cite":"[^2] Rose-Hulman lecture 25"},{"text":"At standstill there is no back-emf. Only the resistance limits the current, so start it through its starter.","cite":"[^1] [^17] PEG-3722; CED Engineering"},{"text":"Swapping the supply leads does not reverse it. Swap the field or the armature leads.","cite":"[^15] [^5] AAST; CED Engineering"},{"text":"Isolate, lock off and prove dead. Then megger to frame, measure field and armature resistance, and check the brushes and commutator.","cite":"[^18] [^8] [^19] Pump & Motor Works"}],"where":"Crane hoists, lifts, large excavators and traction.","figures":[]},{"kind":"show","name":"How it works","text":"You already know one. An angle grinder's motor is a series motor built for AC too.\n\nThe field winding is in series with the armature, so one current flows through both. More current means a stronger field and much more torque.\n\nA turning armature also makes a back-emf that opposes the supply. More field or more speed makes more of it. At standstill there is none, so the starting current is large.\n\nRunning, the back-emf sits just under the supply voltage: E = V − I R.","svg":null,"alt":"DC supply series field a few turns of thick wire the same current through both armature turning back-emf","html":"<div class=\"pz\" id=\"pz\"><div id=\"prog\"></div><div id=\"stepbox\"><div id=\"hwW\"><svg class=\"fig k-fig\" id=\"hw\" viewBox=\"0 0 340 222\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"One loop: a DC supply, then the series field, drawn as a few turns of thick wire, then the armature between its two brushes. Arrows on the wires show the same current through both. A curved arrow shows the armature turning. Beside it, an arrow labelled back-emf points against the current the supply drives through it.\" style=\"--k-fig-max: 493px;\"><path d=\"M30 40V100M30 112V184H236V148M30 40H74M134 40H236V76\" class=\"w\"></path><path d=\"M16 100H44\" class=\"plt\"></path><path d=\"M23 112H37\" class=\"plt2\"></path><text x=\"42\" y=\"140\" class=\"lb k-halo\" text-anchor=\"start\">DC supply</text><path d=\"M74 40a6 6 0 0 1 12 0a6 6 0 0 1 12 0a6 6 0 0 1 12 0a6 6 0 0 1 12 0a6 6 0 0 1 12 0\" class=\"coilF\"></path><text x=\"104\" y=\"20\" class=\"lb k-halo\" text-anchor=\"middle\">series field</text><text x=\"44\" y=\"64\" class=\"lb k-halo\" text-anchor=\"start\">a few turns of thick wire</text><polygon points=\"196 40 186 35 186 45\" class=\"ia\"></polygon><polygon points=\"134 184 144 179 144 189\" class=\"ia\"></polygon><text x=\"132\" y=\"206\" class=\"lb k-halo\" text-anchor=\"middle\">the same current through both</text><circle cx=\"236\" cy=\"112\" r=\"28\" class=\"arm\"></circle><g transform=\"translate(236 112)\"><rect x=\"-2\" y=\"-26\" width=\"4\" height=\"6\" rx=\"1\" class=\"slot\" transform=\"rotate(0)\"></rect><rect x=\"-2\" y=\"-26\" width=\"4\" height=\"6\" rx=\"1\" class=\"slot\" transform=\"rotate(45)\"></rect><rect x=\"-2\" y=\"-26\" width=\"4\" height=\"6\" rx=\"1\" class=\"slot\" transform=\"rotate(90)\"></rect><rect x=\"-2\" y=\"-26\" width=\"4\" height=\"6\" rx=\"1\" class=\"slot\" transform=\"rotate(135)\"></rect><rect x=\"-2\" y=\"-26\" width=\"4\" height=\"6\" rx=\"1\" class=\"slot\" transform=\"rotate(180)\"></rect><rect x=\"-2\" y=\"-26\" width=\"4\" height=\"6\" rx=\"1\" class=\"slot\" transform=\"rotate(225)\"></rect><rect x=\"-2\" y=\"-26\" width=\"4\" height=\"6\" rx=\"1\" class=\"slot\" transform=\"rotate(270)\"></rect><rect x=\"-2\" y=\"-26\" width=\"4\" height=\"6\" rx=\"1\" class=\"slot\" transform=\"rotate(315)\"></rect></g><rect x=\"230\" y=\"76\" width=\"12\" height=\"8\" rx=\"1.5\" class=\"brush\"></rect><rect x=\"230\" y=\"140\" width=\"12\" height=\"8\" rx=\"1.5\" class=\"brush\"></rect><text x=\"200\" y=\"116\" class=\"lb k-halo\" text-anchor=\"end\">armature</text><path d=\"M268.9 93.0A38 38 0 0 1 268.9 131.0\" class=\"turn\"></path><polygon points=\"264.2 137.4 264.1 128.3 273.7 133.8\" class=\"ia\"></polygon><text x=\"262\" y=\"166\" class=\"lb k-halo\" text-anchor=\"start\">turning</text><line x1=\"300\" y1=\"144\" x2=\"300.0\" y2=\"97.0\" class=\"emf\"></line><polygon points=\"300.0 88.0 305.0 97.0 295.0 97.0\" class=\"emfh\"></polygon><text x=\"300\" y=\"76\" class=\"lb k-halo\" text-anchor=\"middle\">back-emf</text></svg></div></div></div>","h":237},{"kind":"show","name":"One current: field, torque, speed","text":"At full load the field, torque and speed all read 1.00. At twice the current, as at a hard start, the torque is 4.00 and the speed 0.50. At half the current the torque is 0.25 and the speed 2.00. At a quarter, the torque is only 0.06 and the speed 4.00, deep in the red. The back-emf bar stays full throughout. The speed crosses the example danger line near a third of full-load current.","svg":null,"alt":"Assume an ideal series motor, with no saturation and no resistance. 1.00 is full load. The red line at 3.00 is an example, not a maker's limit. DC supply series field field back-emf the same current through both armature load gears hoist drum speed danger line: an example, not a maker's 0 1.00 2.00 3.00 4.00 0.25 0.50 1.00 1.50 2.00 Torque and speed, per unit Load current, per unit speed torque Field, per unit 0.25 Torque, per unit 0.06 Speed, per unit 4.00 At 0.25 of full-load current the field is 0.25. Torque is field times current, so it is 0.06. The back-emf must still match the supply.","html":"<div class=\"pz\" id=\"pz\"><div id=\"prog\"></div><div id=\"stepbox\" class=\"enter\"><p class=\"why\" id=\"pre\">Assume an ideal series motor, with no saturation and no resistance. 1.00 is full load. The red line at 3.00 is an example, not a maker's limit.</p><div class=\"live\" id=\"live\"><div class=\"lcol\"><svg class=\"fig k-fig\" id=\"mot\" viewBox=\"0 0 340 210\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"The series motor's loop, small: a DC supply, the series field and the armature, the same current through both. A bar beside the field shows its strength, and a bar below shows the back-emf, which stays full. The armature turns, slowed right down, and drives the hoist drum through a pair of gears. A load hangs on the drum's rope, its size in step with the torque.\" style=\"--k-fig-max: 493px;\"><path d=\"M26 34V92M26 104V176H176V138M26 34H70M130 34H176V70\" class=\"w\"></path><path d=\"M12 92H40\" class=\"plt\"></path><path d=\"M19 104H33\" class=\"plt2\"></path><text x=\"38\" y=\"136\" class=\"lb k-halo\" text-anchor=\"start\">DC supply</text><path d=\"M70 34a6 6 0 0 1 12 0a6 6 0 0 1 12 0a6 6 0 0 1 12 0a6 6 0 0 1 12 0a6 6 0 0 1 12 0\" class=\"coilF\"></path><text x=\"100\" y=\"14\" class=\"lb k-halo\" text-anchor=\"middle\">series field</text><rect x=\"70\" y=\"46\" width=\"60\" height=\"10\" rx=\"2\" class=\"barO\"></rect><rect x=\"70\" y=\"46\" width=\"7.5\" height=\"10\" rx=\"2\" class=\"barF\"></rect><text x=\"136\" y=\"55\" class=\"lb k-halo\" text-anchor=\"start\">field</text><rect x=\"46\" y=\"148\" width=\"60\" height=\"10\" rx=\"2\" class=\"barO\"></rect><rect x=\"46\" y=\"148\" width=\"60\" height=\"10\" rx=\"2\" class=\"barE\"></rect><text x=\"112\" y=\"157\" class=\"lb k-halo\" text-anchor=\"start\">back-emf</text><polygon points=\"162 34 152 29 152 39\" class=\"ia\"></polygon><polygon points=\"104 176 114 171 114 181\" class=\"ia\"></po